    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-274609",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
