    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-30588",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine",
      "problem": "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$",
